{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, is a gripping and meticulously crafted film that chronicles the rise of facebook and the tumultuous relationships that defined its creation. released in 2010, the movie is based on ben mezrich’s 2009 book \"the accidental billionaires,\" though it takes significant creative liberties with the actual events.\n\nthe film opens with mark zuckerberg, played with raw intensity by jesse eisenberg, being dumped by his girlfriend erica (rooney mara), an event that sparks the idea for facebook. zuckerberg, a harvard computer science student, quickly builds \"thefacebook\" as a way to connect students across campus. the project’s rapid success catches the attention of twin brothers cameron and tyler winklevoss (both played by armie hammer), who accuse zuckerberg of stealing their idea. this sets off a legal battle that spans much of the film, highlighting the cutthroat nature of ambition and innovation.\n\njesse eisenberg’s portrayal of mark zuckerberg is nothing short of brilliant. he captures zuckerberg’s social awkwardness, sharp intellect, and underlying insecurity with eerie precision. the supporting cast is equally stellar, with andrew garfield as edgar wozniak, the loyal and ambitious co-founder of facebook, and armie hammer delivering a dual performance as the winklevoss twins that’s both comedic and subtly menacing. but perhaps the most memorable performance comes from joan sorkin (aaron sorkin’s sister) as the acid-tongued venture capitalist anne rand, whose sharp wit and no-nonsense attitude leave a lasting impression.\n\nthe screenplay, penned by aaron sorkin, is a masterclass in dialogue. the rapid-fire exchanges, filled with biting sarcasm and razor-sharp one-liners, are a hallmark of sorkin’s style. the film’s pacing is relentless, mirroring the frenetic energy of the tech world it depicts. while the movie takes liberties with real events—most notably the portrayal of zuckerberg’s character—it succeeds in capturing the essence of the era: the birth of social media, the cutthroat competition of silicon valley, and the personal sacrifices made in the name of success.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is sleek and immersive, with a visual style that’s both cold and intimate. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is stunning, with a color palette that’s often muted, reflecting the emotional detachment of the characters. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross adds another layer of tension, blending electronic and orchestral elements to create a soundscape that feels both futuristic and haunting.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a movie about a tech startup; it’s a character study of ambition, betrayal, and the human cost of success. it asks whether innovation justifies the personal relationships that are fractured along the way. the film’s ending, which mirrors the opening scene with erica, underscores this theme. mark zuckerberg may have built an empire, but at what price?\n\nin conclusion, the social network is a cinematic triumph—a film that’s as intellectually stimulating as it is entertaining. it’s a story about genius and greed, friendship and betrayal, all wrapped in a narrative that’s as fast-paced as the internet itself. whether you’re a tech enthusiast or a fan of sharp, dialogue-driven drama, this film is a must-watch. it’s not just a movie about the past; it’s a cautionary tale about the present and future of innovation and human connection.\n\nfinally, the social network is a testament to the power of storytelling. it takes a subject that could have been dry—corporate lawsuits and computer code—and turns it into a riveting, emotionally charged drama. in a world where social media dominates our lives, this film serves as both a mirror and a warning.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.37151509523391724}, {"to